    <item>
      <title>NASW-Metro DC Book Club Event at Lindsay Hall (Inabel Burns Lindsay Hall - School of Social Work)</title>
      <description><![CDATA[<div>  <img src="https://localist-images.azureedge.net/photos/53878739780687/huge/c7ee13c7f8082414aa3fdac705efe23540d1f169.jpg" style="width: 100%;" />  <div style="font-size: 0.9em; color: #555;"><strong>Tue, 22 Sep 2026</strong></div>  <div><p>Celebrate the release and impact of Accountable Care and Identity Camaraderie for Transgender and Queer Service Providers at a special NASW Metro DC Book Club Author Talk &amp; Signing hosted at the Howard University School of Social Work. </p>

<p> </p>

<p>Written by Howard University School of Social Work alum Dr. Shanéa Thomas, LICSW, CSE, the book explores identity, accountability, community care, and affirming practices for transgender and queer service providers, offering valuable insights for social workers, educators, students, and helping professionals. The evening will feature a moderated conversation with the author, exploring the inspiration behind the book, its central themes, and its relevance to contemporary social work practice, followed by audience questions and a book signing.</p>

<p><a href="https://events.howard.edu/event/nasw-metro-dc-book-club-event">View on site</a> | <a href="mailto:?subject=I found an interesting event%3A NASW-Metro DC Book Club Event&amp;body=I found an interesting event you may like%3A%0A%0A%0ADate%3A Sep 22%2C 2026%0A%0ADescription%3A%0ACelebrate the release and impact of Accountable Care and Identity Camaraderie for Transgender and Queer Service Providers at a special NASW Metro DC Book Club Author Talk %26 Signing hosted at the Howard University School of Social Work. %0A%0A %0A%0AWritten by Howard University School of Social Work alum Dr. Shan%C3%A9a Thomas%2C LICSW%2C CSE%2C the book explores identity%2C accountability%2C community care%2C and affirming practices for transgender and queer service providers%2C offering valuable insights for social workers%2C educators%2C students%2C and helping professionals. The evening will feature a moderated conversation with the author%2C exploring the inspiration behind the book%2C its central themes%2C and its relevance to contemporary social work practice%2C followed by audience questions and a book signing.%0A%0Ahttps%3A%2F%2Fevents.howard.edu%2Fevent%2Fnasw-metro-dc-book-club-event%0A">Email this event</a></p></div></div>]]></description>
